# Inactive admission-owned v2 marker — isolated SQL composition

Owned source: `src/lib/ai/conversation/__tests__/p5-voice-v2-admission-marker-sql.test.ts` only. The file is frozen. No existing test, SQL, migration or setting was edited by this test task.

The first focused execution passed **36/36 cases**, zero skips, in 10.46 seconds. Initial strict TypeScript and scoped ESLint logs are empty/pass. No fixture, implementation assertion or SQL constraint was corrected or relaxed to obtain this result. Logs remain `marker-sql-initial.log/json`, `marker-sql-typecheck-initial.log`, and `marker-sql-lint-initial.log`.

The fixture composes real unchanged P3b AES-GCM/HMAC, the completed verifier, actual request-owned admission and new marker orchestration, protected v2 parent/current/cleanup/proof/capacity/marker transports, and existing exact capacity close. It executes the preserved SQL draft chain only in PostgreSQL 18.6 through PGlite 0.5.12. Initial authority creation is deliberately a manual real seal plus actual v2 issuance registration; it does not execute the complete trusted v2 issuer orchestration. Auth/policy/approval metadata and transport/clock hooks are fictional local fixtures.

Coverage:

- One separate fresh unused inspect proof passes directly from registration to marker; ordinary admission inspection consumes its own distinct receipt. Marker adds no quota debit or reservation.
- Private immutable claim/cleanup/Auth-parent/fingerprint ownership, retained operation ID before the first Auth wait, exact expected pool version and all full request/acknowledgment fields.
- A marker after 40 seconds uses new conservative verification while retaining the original 60-second claim lifetime; previous shorter Auth/approval observations remain ceilings.
- Parent/approval revocation, approval replacement, thread revision, allowlist withdrawal and Auth-session changes after proof registration and after marker commit.
- Actual SQL pool-version, disabled-pool, quarantine and disabled-quota denials, and an actual already-marked acknowledgment that never yields a new marked observation.
- Lost/null/mixed protocol, operation, pool version, proof receipt and proof-time replies after committed marking; no retry, replacement identity, fallback, automatic close or refund.
- Original proof expiry at registration and marker return, post-registration shortening, same-handle overlapping markers/revalidation, close before delayed proof or marker SQL, and close during a committed marker reply.
- Explicit exact cleanup after lost close acknowledgment, including foreign Auth/unsafe local time; marked allocations retain occupied slots and cumulative holds pending authenticated whole-operation finality.
- Concurrent JavaScript handles preserve distinct operation/proof/owner associations. Transactions are intentionally serialized on the single local connection, so this is not native concurrency evidence.

The success case compares all 22 lifecycle/accounting tables and preserves unrelated same-owner historical holds and saved fictional text, allowing only the expected target quota/pool/high-water changes. Other cases independently verify exact allocation identity and target held/occupied accounting. Both engine instances close; Date mocks and the original SQL clock restore. No provider/device, connected SQL, real Auth, runtime route, publication or paid call occurs.

Next meaningful integration evidence: compose the complete trusted v2 issuer with admission and marker through actual transports/SQL, including sequential new immutable authorities under the same genuine fictional Auth parent, separate fresh attempt approvals, retained uncertain marked holds, and no cross-authority adoption. That would fill the current manually sealed/registered fixture gap without repeating completed models/contracts. A separate offline cutover preflight can verify inventory/order/privilege expectations; it must not claim deployed routing, quiesced native concurrency, real Auth or provider/device acceptance.

=== marker-implementation-notes.md ===
# Inactive admission-owned v2 capacity marker — implementation notes

Client date: 5 November 2026 America/Toronto. Base main: `4f3aa6a3e89705e635d608fbe40d7e17fdd70f4f`, tree `67027ab4033ee1e8e66488dd5d23fa8beb606bcb` verified by root before the batch.

Owned changes:
- Modify only `src/lib/ai/conversation/service/voice-v2-admission.server.ts`.
- Add only `src/lib/ai/conversation/__tests__/p5-voice-v2-admission-marker.test.ts`.

Added opt-in `createVoiceV2CapacityAdmission` within the same private admission constructor. Original `createVoiceV2Admission` retains its original four-method public surface and does not read marker configuration/ports. Existing admission logic remains in the common private closure; the extension never attaches an arbitrary claim, exposed snapshot or legacy lease. The new factory requires explicit positive safe expected pool version and captures the bound protected marker method/configuration once.

An active nonbusy handle accepts at most one marker attempt. Its synchronous fence precedes UUID allocation and every wait; once started, even an allocation failure cannot trigger a replacement operation. The immutable operation ID, expected pool version and exact private ownership are retained before the first verifier wait. Calls while inactive, busy or carrying extra arguments start no attempt. Concurrent marker/revalidation work shares the existing busy guard, while synchronous close still wins immediately.

The marker uses a separate fresh full protocol-2 inspect proof from the completed real verifier. That proof is registered once and goes exclusively to the marker; ordinary inspect never consumes it first. The full marker request is retained before proof registration, followed by exact full registration ACK validation and fresh protected verification under the original proof's deadline. The full marker transport receives the exact proof, immutable operation ID and configured version once. Complete ACK parsing and field comparisons reject mixed/legacy/peeled/mismatched fields, including all nested proof identity/time data.

A matching late ACK is retained as content-free evidence before local cancellation/expiry checks. Only status `marked`, followed by fresh same-association verification within the same original proof deadline and shrink-only lifecycle ceiling, returns an offline observation. `already-marked`, denied, quarantined and closed ACKs remain evidence but return null and do not permit any provider create or replay. Null/lost/malformed replies never imply rollback. Failure terminally invalidates local admission, clears its retained opaque request and leaves exact cleanup ownership plus marker evidence. No automatic retry, replacement ID, new marker proof, cleanup call or refund occurs. Explicit cleanup remains independent of current Auth, clock or eligibility and preserves marked uncertain accounting through the existing SQL semantics.

The marker snapshot retains immutable operation/configuration/ownership, request, registration attempt/ACK and marker attempt/ACK separately from ordinary admission operations. Snapshots are detached/frozen content-free last observations; no envelope, writing, credential, raw error or provider capability is exposed. Successful markers neither extend the original claim nor adopt the verifier's 30-second freshness window as the lifecycle deadline. Later observations can only shorten effective eligibility.

Validation:
- Initial focused run: 154/154 pass across two files = 77 new marker cases plus 77 unchanged original admission cases (`marker-primary-initial.log`).
- Scoped ESLint for modified source/new primary test passes (`marker-primary-lint.log`).
- No initial primary implementation/test failures or corrections; no assertions, timing bounds or SQL constraints weakened.
- Independent review reports six cases passed first run and no source blocker; SQL tests and root aggregate are separately owned.

Primary coverage includes real preserved local AES-GCM/HMAC with a fictional secret, full actual verifier, new-proof exclusive marker routing, exact immutable echo/ACK fields, captured ports/version, malformed/getter inputs, already-marked and denial handling, lost/late replies, shortening, original proof freshness across postregistration waits, monotonic clock failure, +40-second later marker under the unchanged 90-second claim, concurrent-call suppression and synchronous close with retained late evidence. Explicit local clock/Auth/response doubles are not deployed Auth, native cross-backend concurrency, HTTP fault injection or provider/device acceptance.

Frozen SHA-256:
- `src/lib/ai/conversation/service/voice-v2-admission.server.ts`: `9375cab5bb5e066140efd2e78f149e24818f480282ca04709b66cc31e9985380`
- `src/lib/ai/conversation/__tests__/p5-voice-v2-admission-marker.test.ts`: `4447ef826372662541f256f23711fb9b0fd7bd9f6a52aff14166943ce052025b`

Concrete remaining offline gap: the actual v2 issuer SQL suite and admission/marker SQL suites currently exercise separate compositions. A bounded end-to-end test should start with the actual trusted issuer and fresh owned service instance, carry its returned exact envelope into real v2 admission/marker transports on one isolated database, then verify cancellation/revocation/cleanup and retained marked holds. Do not replace this with another generic contract wrapper or new protocol. Separately, exact legacy/v2 enabling RPC privilege/route cutover remains required before deployment; a read-only source/SQL inventory can prepare the future quiesced acceptance checklist without applying connected SQL or claiming live acceptance.
